<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
        audit_log_entries.actor_user_id becomes nullable, and null now means
        "the platform itself did this".

        Found by the reservation expiry sweep, not by inspection. Every audit
        entry written until now came from a request, so "some human did this"
        was a safe assumption and NOT NULL encoded it. The first scheduled job
        in this system breaks it: a hold expiring is nobody's action. TX-5
        says so explicitly — the actor is the system, not the buyer.

        The alternatives were worse. Writing the buyer's id would state in the
        permanent record that they released a plot they did not touch, which
        is precisely the misattribution an audit trail exists to prevent, and
        a disputed allocation is exactly when someone would read it. A
        sentinel "system user" row would put a login-shaped record in `users`
        that nobody can log in as, and which every user-facing count and
        listing would then have to remember to exclude.

        The read path renders null as "System" rather than "Unknown user":
        an entry whose actor was deleted and an entry that never had one are
        different facts, and the reader deserves to see which is which.
    -->
    <changeSet id="055-allow-system-actor-in-audit-log" author="landvault">
        <sql><![CDATA[
            ALTER TABLE audit_log_entries ALTER COLUMN actor_user_id DROP NOT NULL;

            COMMENT ON COLUMN audit_log_entries.actor_user_id IS
                'The user who performed the action, or NULL when the platform itself did — a '
                'scheduled job with no human behind it, such as a reservation hold expiring. '
                'Deliberately not a sentinel user id: an action nobody took should read as such, '
                'and attributing it to the affected user would misrepresent the record.';
        ]]></sql>
        <rollback>
            <!--
                Only succeeds while no system-authored entry exists. Once the
                expiry sweep has run even once, rolling back means deleting
                those rows first — which an append-only table forbids. That
                is inherent to the change, not a defect in it: the point of
                the column being nullable is that some entries genuinely have
                no actor.
            -->
            ALTER TABLE audit_log_entries ALTER COLUMN actor_user_id SET NOT NULL;
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
